Splitting a partition divides a partition into two partitions. You can split a partition using the ALTER TABLE command. You can split partitions only at the lowest level of your partition hierarchy (partitions that contain data). For a multi-level partition, only range partitions can be split, not list partitions. The split value you specify goes into the latter partition. For example, to split a monthly partition into two with the first partition containing dates January 1-15 and the second partition containing dates January 16-31: ALTER TABLE sales SPLIT PARTITION FOR ('2017-01-01')
AT ('2017-01-16')
INTO (PARTITION jan171to15, PARTITION jan1716to31); If your partition design has a default partition, you must split the default partition to add a partition. When using the INTO clause, specify the current default partition as the second partition name. For example, to split a default range partition to add a new monthly partition for January 2017: ALTER TABLE sales SPLIT DEFAULT PARTITION START ('2017-01-01') INCLUSIVE END ('2017-02-01') EXCLUSIVE INTO (PARTITION jan17, default partition);

