Parameters passed to functions are named with identifiers such as $1, $2. Optionally, aliases can be declared for $n parameter names for increased readability. Either the alias or the numeric identifier can then be used to refer to the parameter value. There are two ways to create an alias. The preferred way is to give a name to the parameter in the CREATE FUNCTION command, for example: CREATE FUNCTION sales_tax(subtotal real) RETURNS real AS $$
BEGIN RETURN subtotal * 0.06;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql; You can also explicitly declare an alias, using the declaration syntax: name ALIAS FOR $n; This example, creates the same function with the DECLARE syntax. CREATE FUNCTION sales_tax(real) RETURNS real AS $$
DECLARE subtotal ALIAS FOR $1;
BEGIN RETURN subtotal * 0.06;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

